\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 By mid week, Chanta had started sulking around in the provisioning floor of his Starborne One bunker. He walked among the logistically scaled aisles of toilet paper, soap and ammunition. He wandered past the lockers of the ten thousand sides of bacon. He plucked bananas, ate them and left their skins on the floor in case any cartoon characters broke in.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 He sat in sports cars and avoided forklifts. He wandered down aisles of medical supplies. The \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 bandages, creams, unguents and toenail clippers made him think of Sarah, of the time after the explosion at the Stickett Inn, and the escape from the hospital, and how she came to his apartment and took care of him.\
He jankled the keys of a grand piano and wondered what it would be like to be able to play one. He mused about the respect a grand pianist probably got, then shrugged that off; respect never made anyone happy.\
He walked past bays of expensive cologne and thought of Sarah, and thought of her again when passed down a liquor aisle and came upon pallets of vodka. Sarah had given him that brand, the day she had brought him socks and cologne. Those were the best of days, even if he was holed up recovering from being singed like a blue steak.\
Sarah had healed him of his burns too, somehow, with the virus he\'92d been carrying since the morning after the Vegas show.\
Chanta shook his head clear of her; he was wandering the bunker to shut the world out, not let Sarah in.\
The next aisle was stocked with beers, even the cheap brands Abe and Major liked.\
\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
An aisle of kitchen equipment\'97chef\'92s knives, carving knives, steak knives, cleavers, sharpening steels\'97reminded him of Halo. Sarah was right about Halo. And wrong too.\
\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 He found vast chill rooms, and got hungry. He ate cheese and salami, and spat out anchovies.\
He feel asleep in the back of a vintage limousine. When he woke, he mounted a motor cycle and, for a lark, wondered if he could get it started.\
He got it started.\
And went on a stately of the bunker until halfway down the first aisle, when he imagined the whole bunker was a speed way. Eventually he pushed his luck taking a turn half way down a cross aisle. The back tyre slid out on the smooth warehouse floor and skidded much more spectacularly than Chanta knew a forklift could. When he got out from under the bike and stood up, Chanta slipped on a banana peel.\
Chastened, but on the bike again, he toured more cautiously, until he came upon the aisle of lockers where the Elvi had been stored in stasis, still in mint packaging. Fisher had assured him all the Elvi had been revived and taken to the ghetto for orientation; they\'92d all been feted and celebrated as OG clones, and had taken to normal Elvi life and culture like ducks to a nightclub.\
Still, Chanta wanted to check. Down the aisle he checked stasis lockers at random. They were all empty.\
That made him think of the Agent Jones Clones.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Chanta stood outside one of the stasis lockers that had contained Jones clones. Then he stood inside one and saw cardboard coffins painted for slogans and consumer appeal. They were all still there, and in the next one too.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Chanta was pretty sure not a single Jones clone had been revived. Part of him was glad; a Jones clone had terrorised him and torn up his favourite Elvi bar to find a thing that turns out to be as common as bar fridges. Yeah, he knew stasis units chilled nothing, but the arithmetic still held, as far as he was concerned.\
He decided to leave the clones in stasis and was only two steps out in the aisle when he hesitated.\
They couldn\'92t be all bad, could they? Right from the get go? Surely being that Bond-villain evil required at least a trade certificate, or torturing as a toddler. People weren\'92t intrinsically evil, Chanta firmly believed. There were no evil Cloudborne, certainly\'97just people who were getting by in bad air that poisoned life and possibility out of everything. Cloudborne weren\'92t bad people.\
Airborne? Chanta didn\'92t even believe they started out bad, or greedy, or naturally gifted at property development whose foundations went straight through pubs and schools and rugby grounds and the one surviving tree in a neighbourhood as if they should have just got up and moved if they found progress a little too inconvenient. That\'92s it\'97expecting trees to pull stumps and get out of the way?\'85 no one is born with those kinds of expectations. You have to be offered the privilege of wanting it first. You have to have heart scooped out with silver spoons and sent to private school to be oriented from arse to arse hole.\
The Jones clones were people too, weren\'92t they? The Elvi were certainly people\'97eccentric, yes, and hard to distinguish among them on first sight, but how is that a scheme for defining an underclass? The Elvi were people, and so were their ancestors, all the way back to the clones and the OG Elvis and his momma Gladys and poppa Vernon and even Jesse was people too.\
Chanta thought for a moment then decided. He needed a hobby.
\f2\fs26 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 If Fisher didn\'92t exactly disagree with Chanta\'97\'93You are a great humanitarian, sir\'94\'97he rallied all his skills in diplomacy and institutionalised intransigence to\'97if not divert Chanta from his plan\'97then delay it until Chanta was given something else to play with. Something safer, like a rusty chainsaw and a wildcard entry to a talent show.
\f2\fs26 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Unfortunately, Chanta countered Fisher\'92s manoeuvres with. \'93You are people too, Fisher. Now look at those boxes and tell me why the clones inside them aren\'92t.\'94\
Fisher considered his options for more than a few moments then answered, \'93Fuck!\'94\
You could have cut the air easier with Fisher\'92s embarrassment than with a light sabre\'97a weapon which Hardy Industries had actually developed back in his heyday. But the contraption failed to sell, mostly because a combatant that was struck by one smelled too much like suckling pig. Worse, troops started to carry bottles of apple sauce into battle, if only to give the fog of war more of a home cookin\'92 atmosphere. A salivating soldier, contrary to the efforts of the best defence force academies, turned out to be an ineffective soldier.\
\'93I mean, tax it all to hell, sir,\'94 Fisher said after recovering.\
\'93So no argument, from you?\'94\
\'93It seems you have outmanoeuvred me, sir.\'94\
\'93Think nothing of it, Fisher. Now, how long before we can get all these guys thawed or cooked or de-programmed, or whatever it is you do?\'94\
Fisher swallowed hard. \'93Might I suggest we start with just one Jones clone, so that we can test the hypothesis that they aren\'92t already single-minded homicidal maniacs?\'94\
\'93It\'92s okay, Fisher. It\'92s not like we\'92re letting them out on the street straight away.\'94\
They bartered until Chanta was delighted he\'92d negotiated Fisher up to microwaving one for a good start.\
\'93See, Fisher. Now that\'92s some artful negotiating there.\'94\
\'93Yes, sir.\'94\
\'93I wonder if I could get a gig as the dude who runs an embassy somewhere.\'94\
\'93That would be an ambassador.\'94\
Chanta snapped his fingers. \'93Exactly! Perhaps I could be ambassador for a tropical island or the place where they have those dog-sled races.\'94\
\'93With respect, sir, if you were made ambassador of chook raffles it would still be catastrophic.\'94\
That rolled straight off Chanta. \'93Thanks for the advice, Fisher.\'94\
\'93To be clear, I was advising that sir should 
\f3\i not
\f1\i0  become an ambassador of chook raffles.\'94\
\'93Really? Oh, well, it\'92s still good advice. Okay, let\'92s start with one of the pink ties. What do you think?\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Over the next days, Fisher relented in the face of Chanta\'92s newfound energy for a hobby. By the end of the second day, there were two Jones clones that went about the penthouse, standing discreetly in corners with their hands clasped in front of them. Occasionally they stared out the windows, inspected the contents of tea caddies and ice cream tubs. They unscrewed lightbulbs and examined them thoroughly. On the third day, the staff could hear exclamations of, \'93Clear!\'94 and \'93The cheese is in the kitchen,\'94 from various parts of the house. One of the four of day three\'92s contingent insisted on doing the cheese\'92s laundry. Despite terse requests like, \'93You will provide me with the detergent\'94, the staff generally agreed that it was good that no one had been murdered yet.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 That night, Chanta taught them to play pool. They were apt students, though Chanta noticed they tended to address the cueball by staring into the corners of the games room ceiling rather than look anywhere on the table.\
None of them showed any interest in alcohol. Or the Elvisettes, who were given to cluck about the square jaws and the obviously buff bods in the severely tailored suits. \
On the fourth day another seven or eight were getting about the penthouse. One insisted on trying to cook, but prepared nothing more than a weapons-grade chilli sauce with a lamb chop in it. By the afternoon another had started slashing pillows in the reception room. The Jones clones were finding listening devices all through the penthouse. While Fisher couldn\'92t stop them outright, they did turn over the bugs for Fisher\'92s inspection. Fisher sighed in relief, and explained those had all been there since Jamison Hardy\'92s day. The Jones clones relaxed and agreed to stop tearing up the furniture, though one took all the bugs to the kitchen and turned them all to fine powder in a blender. The head chef demanded the head of the Jones clone until Fisher directed the clone to go down to the provisions store to retrieve a new blender. The Jones clone snapped to the order, apparently proud to have been sent out on his first mission.\
Chanta lost count of the number of Jones clones working the penthouse by the fifth day. He was, however, sure at least one of every tie colour had been tickled awake. That was something of an achievement, he decided.\
On the seventh day, Fisher came to the gymnasium where Chanta was watching Elvisettes working out extra enthusiastically for the Jones clones who had secured the perimeter.\
\'93What is it, Fisher?\'94 Chanta asked without taking his eyes off the sweating and exercising.\
\'93Sir, staff quarters are at capacity. There are no more beds for the Jones clones.\'94\
\'93I didn\'92t know they slept.\'94\
Fisher recalibrated. \'93Sir. Might I suggest that we bring no more Jones clones up until we are satisfied they are safe to be let out.\'94\
\'93Let out?\'94\
\'93Yes, sir. We can\'92t accommodate them here indefinitely.\'94\
\'93You\'92re right. We should have a party.\'94\
\'93A what, sir?\'94\
\'93A send-off\'97\'94\
\'93No. Sir. It\'92s only been a matter of days\'97a single day for many of them.\'94\
Chanta considered. \'93Tell the staff it\'92s karaoke night. We\'92ll  hold it in reception.\'94\
\'93But, sir. Surely you are not proposing to let them loose in the world just yet?\'94\
\'93Relax, Fisher. If they can\'92t pass karaoke night, they\'92re not ready for the cruelty of the outside world.\'94\
\'93Very good, sir.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Karaoke night started as something of a disappointment. The High Elvis sent his regrets. Sarah claimed she was too tired and something about knowing how Jabba the Hutt felt. Chanta thought she was talking about a song by a band called The Smiths. Kathy was on a well-earned date with Hayley\'97something about a dessert degustation while sky-diving\'97Hayley must have talked Kathy down from a night of less predictability and more accident insurance. Major and Abe wouldn\'92t be caught dead in that part of town. Halo\'92s answering service surprised Chanta by still being in operation. Lonnie was doing a double shift. Th\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
e Very Arch Ignatius d\'92Molesto begged off, saying the evening didn\'92t sound \'93iniquitous enough\'94.\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 At no point did Chanta consider inviting Johnny.\
Once the evening got underway, it took very little time for everyone to figure out that the Jones clones would fail karaoke. While the Elvi staff were obvious naturals, as long as they didn\'92t risk any harmonies, the Jones clones were\'85\
It would be inaccurate to report that the Jones clones couldn\'92t sing, mostly because, by the adjudication of staff, they attempted no singing at all. What they attempted, while struggling to read the cues off the monitor, was more like speaking with a kind of town-cryer voice which, either by because of stress or stroke, they animated with grotesque grimaces and nausea-inducing eye-rolls. On two occasions, a Jones performer tried to hit the high notes by choking himself with his own tie. Another stood on one leg for the entire time he was on stage. Mercifully, Chanta excused them from further calls to sing and allowed them to patrol the perimeter of the room. The evening proceeded as Elvi staff provided the actual entertainment, and a number of Elvisettes performed brave renditions that everyone hoped were not being recorded on devices.\
The Elvisettes. While the night cycled through torch songs, anthems and comedy pieces, some of the Elvisettes started gossiping about the Jones clones. A bet needed settling. Eventually, one Elvisette had been prescribed enough powder and vodka for the bravery she needed to walk up to a Jone clone who was standing with his back to the wall, hands clasped in front. She came to his side, close enough to nuzzle his shoulder, the slowly reached explore the contents of his underwear, which she did with increasing precision over an interval of five or six seconds, during which the Jones remained stoic, and did not even unclasp his hands. After conducting a thorough investigation, the Elvisette declared, \'93He\'92s got a torpedo,\'94 and ran back to her seat.\
The winners who took short odds on \'93Equipped\'94,  rejoiced and downed their vodkas.\
The losers, who backed \'93Ken Doll\'94,  lamented.\
And downed their vodkas.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 The party went well into the early hours of the morning. The Elvisettes felt increasingly safe with the stoic Jones clones and became increasingly handsy with them. The staff found an admiration for Chanta they had never experiences in the time of Jamison Hardy.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 As Chanta announced he would turn in and proclaimed the party should continue, Fisher approached him.\
\'93So should we turn the Jones clones out on the street when the party is over?\'94 he asked Chanta.\
\'93Tax, no!\'94 Chanta said on unsteady feet. \'93They\'92s get eaten alive out there. First we\'92ve gotta school them on some social skills.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
